														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4476016">
									

<p>I kinda like the idea of the SCP here: my -1 was more for the execution. Specifically, my big issue is that the real meat of this article is, I guess, the testing logs and they really don't reveal much to the reader beyond how the Foundation figured out how it works; and even then it feels very much like the examples provided wouldn't be sufficient. Like, the way Campbell figures it out feels like it only works if you already know how it works? But as a second test, all it's really established is &quot;the majority of the body remains alive&quot;.</p>
<p>This wouldn't be a fatal flaw, except it's followed by an Addendum of the SCP being used where the one actually interesting thing that happens is redacted instead of having some really fun, vivid imagery; and then an Addendum featuring relatively generic pleas for help.</p>
<p>I feel like this would work better if the article was written in a lot more of a provisional state: where the description admits the Foundation doesn't exactly know what the sword does so in turn a reader doesn't exactly know what the sword does; and then you can have a bunch of testing and like gradually reveal it so the reader can piece together what it does as the Foundation does too. Or alternatively: if the reader knows exactly what the sword does and is forced to watch in horror as the Foundation tests hundreds of things in a desperate attempt to figure it out.</p>

							</div>

								
					
